<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/686">686</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ubayd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Ziyad was an Umayyad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi">Ziyad ibn Abihi</a>. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a>, he sent the army that killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> and his followers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680, which made him one of the most reviled figures in Shia tradition. He was killed in 686 at the Battle of the Khazir River by the forces of al-Mukhtar, who sought vengeance for Husayn.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad.jpg" alt="Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_Dirham_in_the_name_of_Ubayd_Allah_ibn_Ziyad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ubayd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi">Ziyad ibn Abihi</a>, whose tribal origins were obscure, and of a Persian concubine named Murjanah. His father governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Khurasan for Caliph Mu'awiya I and prepared his son to succeed him. After Ziyad's death Ubayd Allah became governor of Khurasan, and a year or two later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. The historian Hugh N. Kennedy judged him more hasty and given to force than his father, but wholly devoted to the Umayyad cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 674 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and defeated the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, the first known Arab Muslim invasion of the city, and took several thousand Bukharan captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> as slaves, from whom the Bukhariya military unit was formed. He struck coins in his name on the Sasanian model, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> script. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> was added to his governorship in 679/680, giving him full control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> sent his cousin Muslim ibn Aqil to Kufa, Ubayd Allah won over the Kufan noblemen, and Ibn Aqil was abandoned and slain on 10 September 680.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> in 683, the Basrans turned against Ubayd Allah. An attempt to regain power through the Azdi chieftain Mas'ud ibn Amr failed when Mas'ud was killed in the mosque, and in March 684 Ubayd Allah fled almost alone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, leaving his family behind. There he persuaded the Umayyad elder Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who was about to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, to claim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ubayd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> fought at the Battle of Marj Rahit in August 684, where the Qaysi tribes were routed, and was then given command of Marwan's army, which he rebuilt by recruiting Qaysi tribes. He spent the following year fighting the Qaysi tribes of the Jazira under Zufar ibn al-Harith al-Kilabi, and by 686 his army numbered some 60,000 troops. At the Khazir River he faced Ibrahim ibn al-Ashtar, who led an army composed largely of non-Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, and was routed and slain along with his lieutenants. Abd al-Malik then halted advances against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> until 691. In Muslim tradition Ubayd Allah is remembered above all for the killings of members of Ali's family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During it Husayn ibn Ali, Ali's son and Muhammad's grandson, was killed with a small band of followers at Karbala in 680 by forces sent by Yazid's governor of Iraq, <b>Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Khazir River. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
